# Physics as pure functions

**Topic 02: Physics as pure functions, and a first test**

After this talk you can write a static utility method, test it with JUnit 5, and explain why two doubles are compared with a tolerance, or exactly.

Java skills: static utility classes, `Math`, floating point, JUnit 5, Maven test layout.

---

# A pure function

A pure function returns the same answer for the same arguments and touches nothing else. No fields, no clock, no I/O.

Hadur's physics are all pure functions in two `final` classes with private constructors:

```java
public final class Angles {
    // ...
    private Angles() {}
```

`final` stops subclassing. The private constructor stops anyone creating one. The class is only a home for static methods.

*Source: `hadur2.core.physics.Angles`.*

---

# Angles wrap around

Directions are in radians, and 2 pi is the same direction as 0. Before you compare or subtract angles, normalise them.

```java
public static double normalAbsoluteAngle(double angle) {
    angle %= TWO_PI;
    return angle >= 0 ? angle : angle + TWO_PI;
}
```

Java's `%` keeps the sign of the left operand. So `-1.0 % 6.28` is `-1.0`, not `5.28`. The second line adds one more turn to fix it.

*Source: `Angles.normalAbsoluteAngle`.*

---

# Relative angles: the short way round

An **absolute** angle is in [0, 2 pi). A **relative** angle is in [-pi, pi): the sign says which way to turn, and the size is the short way.

```java
public static double normalRelativeAngle(double angle) {
    angle %= TWO_PI;
    if (angle >= 0) {
        return angle < PI ? angle : angle - TWO_PI;
    }
    return angle >= -PI ? angle : angle + TWO_PI;
}
```

Exactly pi maps to -pi. The interval is closed below and open above, as in the engine.

---

# Bullet speed

A bigger bullet is slower: speed is `20 - 3 x power`.

```java
public static double getBulletSpeed(double power) {
    power = Math.min(Math.max(power, MIN_BULLET_POWER), MAX_BULLET_POWER);
    return 20 - 3 * power;
}
```

| Power | Speed (px/tick) |
|---|---|
| 3.0 | 11 |
| 1.0 | 17 |
| 0.1 | 19.7 |

Power is clamped to [0.1, 3.0] first. Reassigning `power` is safe: it only changes the method's own copy of the argument.

*Source: `hadur2.core.physics.Rules`.*

---

# Maximum escape angle

A robot moves at most 8 px per tick. A bullet of speed `v` takes time to arrive. In that time the target can swing the bullet's aim by at most `asin(8 / v)`.

Hadur's wave stores it when it is created:

```java
this.maxEscapeAngle = Math.asin(8.0 / bulletSpeed);
```

| Power | Bullet speed | Max escape angle |
|---|---|---|
| 3.0 | 11 | about 46.7 degrees |
| 1.0 | 17 | about 28.1 degrees |

Fast bullets leave the target less room. Topic 08 uses this angle as the scale for guess factors.

*Source: `hadur2.core.model.Wave`.*

---

# Damage and energy

```java
public static double getBulletDamage(double power) {
    double damage = 4 * power;
    if (power > 1) {
        damage += 2 * (power - 1);
    }
    return damage;
}
```

A power-3 hit costs `4 x 3 + 2 x 2 = 16`. The shooter gets back `3 x power`. A robot hitting a wall at full speed loses `8 / 2 - 1 = 3.0`, the same as the biggest bullet. Topic 09 shows how the energy ledger has to account for it.

---

# Doubles are not exact

`0.1 + 0.2` is not `0.3` in binary floating point. Compare computed values with a tolerance:

```java
assertEquals(782, end.location.x, 1e-9);
```

The third argument is the allowed difference. Without it, `assertEquals(double, double)` demands exactly the same value.

*Source: `WallStopTest`, which checks that a predicted robot stops at a wall.*

---

# A first test, laid out by Maven

```
hadur-core/
  src/main/java/hadur2/core/physics/Angles.java
  src/test/java/hadur2/core/physics/WallStopTest.java
```

The test sits in the **same package** under `src/test/java`, so it can see package-private code. Surefire runs classes named `*Test`, `*Tests` or `*Properties`.

```java
@Test
@DisplayName("away from walls nothing changes")
void openFieldKeepsSpeed() {
```

`@Test` marks a test method. `@DisplayName` is the sentence in the report.

---

# A property: one rule, thousands of inputs

```java
@Property(tries = 5000)
void normalRelativeAngleMatchesEngine(@ForAll @DoubleRange(min = -1e4, max = 1e4) double a) {
    assertEquals(robocode.util.Utils.normalRelativeAngle(a), Angles.normalRelativeAngle(a));
}
```

jqwik generates 5000 angles. If one fails it shrinks to a small failing case. Note there is **no tolerance**: Hadur's copy must equal the engine's exactly.

*Source: `PhysicsMatchesEngineProperties`.*

---

# Why bit-identical matters

The core may not import `robocode.*`, so it carries its own copy of the engine's maths. If the copy differs in the last bit, a recorded battle replays differently and every behaviour test is suspect.

So the build checks the copy against the engine on thousands of angles and on edge cases: zero, pi, infinities and NaN.

Lab 02 writes this test for your own `Angles`.

*Sources: `Angles` Javadoc, `PhysicsMatchesEngineProperties`.*
